-- # Chapter 0: One Term

-- A Zydeco source file contains one term. This tutorial is a chain of such
-- files, each focused on one aspect of the language. A program does not begin
-- with a distinguished sequence of declarations and end with a `main`
-- declaration. Its outer term describes both its dependencies and its result.

begin
  -- A block is a term inspired by monadic block notation. It gives the
  -- context-aware forms below one region in which their dependencies can be
  -- resolved. The block itself does not decide whether its result is a kind,
  -- type, value, or computation.

  -- `param` turns the block into a function. Here its argument is the canonical
  -- Builtin package: the host types and operations that the launcher supplies.
  -- `that` lets this parameter move within the current block according to the
  -- dependency graph, so later definitions may use its fields.
  param (
    (/core; /representations; /system) :
    @(import("../../lib/std/builtin.zy"))
  ) that
  let (/VType; /Thk; /Ret) = core that
  let (/Scalar = Int64) = representations/i64 that
  let (/Scalar = String) = representations/string that
  let (/process) = system that

  -- A former `data` declaration is now a type-valued definition. Parameters
  -- are ordinary term parameters, and the `data ... end` term constructs the
  -- recursive value type together with its constructors.
  def Product2 (A : VType) (B : VType) =
    data
    | +Pair : A * B
    end
  that

  -- Codata is handled in the same way. It constructs a computation type whose
  -- observations are described by its destructors.
  def CoProduct2 (A : VType) (B : VType) =
    codata
    | .first : Ret A
    | .second : Ret B
    end
  that

  -- `let` gives a transparent definition. Its right-hand side is itself a
  -- parameterized type term, replacing the old type-alias declaration.
  let CoProductInt (A : VType) =
    CoProduct2 Int64 A
  that

  -- `def` seals its right-hand side at the given classifier. This is a value
  -- definition even though that value is a thunk containing a computation.
  def sample : Thk (CoProductInt (Product2 Int64 String)) = {
    comatch
    | .first => ret 0
    | .second => ret +Pair(0, "42")
    end
  } that

  -- The final subterm is the result of the whole block. Because it has type
  -- `OS`, the command boundary can run this file as a program.
  do pair <- ! sample .second;
  match pair
  | +Pair(status, _) =>
    ! (process/exit) status
  end
end

-- The five context-forming ingredients used above are therefore ordinary term
-- composition: `begin ... end`, `param`, transparent `let`, sealed `def`, and
-- the `data` or `codata` terms on their right-hand sides. `that` permits
-- dependency-directed movement inside the inhabiting block; use `in` when a
-- binding should remain in its written lexical tail.

-- Run this file directly:
--
--     cargo run --bin zydeco -- run docs/spell/0-toplevel.zy
--
-- The relative import is resolved from this file, and no project
-- configuration participates in the command.
